---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>
	<body>
		{/* No header bar on the home page: the name in the lead line is the page title. */}
		<main>
			<h1 class="lead">
				I'm <strong>Sean Sobieraj</strong>, a cybersecurity data architect working toward semantic
				models and knowledge graphs.
			</h1>
			<p class="links">
				<a href="/resume/">Resume</a>
				<span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
				<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sobieraj/" rel="me">LinkedIn</a>
				<span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
				<a href="https://github.com/ssobieraj" rel="me">GitHub</a>
			</p>

			<p>
				I work at the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) as a Senior Data
				Architect, leading data management for its Cybersecurity Division. I defined the agency-wide
				tagging and metadata practices, simplified our enterprise data model from hundreds of classes
				to a handful of domains, and architected the production rollout of the Open Cybersecurity Schema
				Framework (OCSF), which now normalizes more than 100 telemetry feeds from federal agencies into
				a common schema.
			</p>
			<p>
				Just as much of the work is cultural: getting teams to build on shared, curated data instead
				of raw feeds, so an analytic written once can run across many sources and be reused by other
				teams.
			</p>
			<p>
				I started out in the U.S. Marine Corps, where I helped maintain a base network for 35,000
				users and rose to sergeant. I earned a B.S. from the Rochester Institute of Technology, then an
				M.S. in Information Security from Purdue University on a federal Scholarship for Service, which
				led me to US-CERT. There I analyzed malware and eventually led the malware analysis team, before
				serving as a Duty Officer in the White House Situation Room at the National Security Council.
			</p>
			<p>
				Back at DHS, I designed and built the malware triage and reporting platform. It cut the time to
				first findings from days to minutes and stayed in use for more than a decade. Later, I led a
				DevOps team building tools for threat hunting, before moving into data architecture.
			</p>
			<p>
				The common thread has been giving security data structure, from US-CERT's first
				machine-readable malware reports to the data models I work on today. Semantic models and
				knowledge graphs are the next step: an explicit map of security entities and how they relate,
				which analysts and AI systems alike can reason over.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.lead {
		font-size: 1.45em;
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 1.35;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
	}
	.lead strong {
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.links {
		margin: 0 0 2.5em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 0.9em;
	}
	.links span {
		margin: 0 0.4em;
	}
	.links a {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		text-decoration: none;
	}
	.links a:hover {
		color: var(--accent-hover);
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
</style>
